      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Most of the driveways we see that "need resurfacing" need a good cleaning, a tube of crack filler and two coats of sealer. A few that were "just going to get sealed" needed a new base. Getting the diagnosis right is worth more than anything you will buy afterwards.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-a-sealer-is" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What a Sealer Is
        </h2>

        <p>
          A driveway sealer is a protective coating, and it is worth being blunt about how thin it is. Whether it is an asphalt emulsion, a coal tar emulsion or an acrylic, it goes on as a liquid and dries to a film somewhere between ten and twenty thousandths of an inch thick per coat. Two coats is less than the thickness of a credit card. Its job is entirely defensive: it blocks ultraviolet light that oxidises the asphalt binder and turns it grey and brittle, it sheds water so less of it gets into the pavement and freezes there, it resists the oil and fuel that soften asphalt, and it makes the driveway look black and cared-for. Done every two to three years on a sound driveway, it can genuinely double the pavement's life. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a> and our <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">sealer recommendations</a> cover the products and the method.
        </p>

        <p>
          What a sealer does not do is repair anything. It does not fill cracks wider than a hairline, it does not level a low spot, it does not bind loose aggregate back into the surface, and it adds no structural strength to the pavement. Pour it into a crack and it dries to a thin skin that tears the first time the crack moves. Spread it over a ravelled, pebbly surface and it coats every loose stone individually, then those stones keep coming loose with a black coating on them. A sealer over a damaged driveway looks better for a month and then looks exactly as damaged as before, only black. This is the single most common disappointment in driveway maintenance, and it happens because the pail says "restores" on the front.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-a-resurfacer-is" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. What a Resurfacer Is
        </h2>

        <p>
          The word resurfacer covers two quite different things, and part of the confusion in this topic is that people use it for both.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">DIY Resurfacer Products</h3>
        <p>
          At retail, a resurfacer is a thick, trowel- or squeegee-grade material that is loaded with aggregate and filler so that it can be applied at an eighth to a quarter of an inch, far thicker than any sealer. Latex-ite Trowel Patch, Gardner's driveway resurfacer products, Sakrete and Quikrete's asphalt-specific patches, and the "resurfacer" or "restorer" grades in the Black Jack line all sit in this group. They are asphalt emulsion binders, usually with a good dose of latex polymer for flexibility, carrying enough sand and mineral filler to have real body. You trowel them into depressions, skim them over ravelled and spider-cracked areas, and feather the edges out into the surrounding asphalt. Once cured, the treated area is a new wearing surface that bridges texture and small defects the way a sealer cannot. These products are genuinely useful within their limits, and the limits are that they are still a surface treatment. They fill and level; they do not add strength, and they cannot fix anything that is moving.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Professional Overlay</h3>
        <p>
          When a paving contractor talks about resurfacing, they mean an overlay: an inch and a half to two inches of new hot mix asphalt laid over the existing driveway with a paver and compacted with a roller. The old surface is cleaned, any failed areas are cut out and patched, the edges are milled or a transition is cut where the new surface meets the garage slab and the street, a tack coat is sprayed to bond the layers, and the new mat goes down hot. The result is effectively a new driveway that uses the old one as its base. It adds real structural thickness, it corrects drainage, and it lasts fifteen years or more if the base underneath is sound. We break down the numbers in <a href="/driveway-resurfacing-cost/">driveway resurfacing cost</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The difference between the two kinds of resurfacing is not just scale. A trowel-grade product is a maintenance repair you can do on a Saturday. An overlay is a construction project with a crew, a paver and a truck of hot asphalt, and it is the answer to a different set of problems.
        </p>

        <h2 id="filler-sealers" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. The Middle Ground: Filler-Sealers
        </h2>

        <p>
          Between the plain sealer and the trowel-grade resurfacer sits a category that the manufacturers label filler-sealer, filled sealer or fortified sealer. These are asphalt emulsions with a heavier load of sand and mineral filler than a standard sealer, so that a squeegee coat lays down a thicker film that partially fills hairline texture and hides minor surface roughness. Most of the premium retail grades, the Latex-ite Optimum and Ultra Shield lines, Black Jack Ultra-Maxx and similar, are in this group, and adding <a href="/sand-additive-for-driveway-sealer/">your own sand</a> to a plain sealer gets you to roughly the same place.
        </p>

        <p>
          A filler-sealer is the right product for a driveway that is a little further along than "oxidised but sound." If the surface has lost some of its fines and has a faintly open, sandy texture, if there are hairline cracks too fine to fill individually, if it has been sealed before and the old coat has worn thin in the tyre tracks, a filler-sealer in two coats will even it out and give a better-looking, longer-lasting result than a plain sealer. What it will not do is fill a depression you can see water standing in, bridge a crack you can push a coin into, or bind a surface that is actively shedding stones. If you are tempted to put three or four coats of filler-sealer on to build it up, stop; that is a resurfacer job and the trowel-grade product will do it better for less money.
        </p>

        <h2 id="diagnosis" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Diagnosing Your Driveway
        </h2>

        <p>
          Walk the driveway after a rain, when the surface is damp and the defects show, and sort what you see into three groups.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Grey but Sound: Seal It</h3>
        <p>
          The asphalt has faded from black to grey. The surface is smooth or has an even, tight texture with the stones firmly held. There are a few hairline cracks and perhaps one or two cracks you could fill with a caulk-style filler. Water runs off and does not pool. When you press a screwdriver into the surface it does not go in and nothing comes loose. This driveway is oxidised, not damaged. Clean it, fill the cracks that need filling with a proper <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">crack filler</a>, and put two coats of a good sealer on. If it has not been sealed in years, the surface may be a little porous and drink the first coat; that is normal.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ravelling, Spider Cracks, Shallow Dips: Resurface It</h3>
        <p>
          The surface is losing its fines and stones are coming loose; you can sweep up a handful of aggregate from the low end of the drive and the texture is open and pebbly. There are networks of fine surface cracks that do not follow a pattern and do not go deep. There are shallow depressions, a half inch or so, where water sits after rain but the asphalt under them is firm when you press it. The edges are wearing but not collapsing. This driveway has a tired surface on a sound base, and that is exactly what a trowel-grade resurfacer is for. Skim the ravelled and spider-cracked areas, fill the dips, feather the edges, let it cure, and then seal over it. A crumbling surface that has gone past this point, where whole patches are coming up, is covered in <a href="/how-to-fix-crumbling-asphalt-driveway/">how to fix a crumbling asphalt driveway</a>. If the whole driveway is in this condition and it is more than fifteen years old, get an overlay quote as well and compare.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Alligatoring, Heaving, Potholes: Overlay or Replace</h3>
        <p>
          The cracks form an interconnected pattern of small polygons like a reptile's back, usually in the tyre paths or where cars sit. Sections have lifted or dropped relative to their neighbours. There are potholes, or patched potholes that have opened up again. The edges have broken away and dropped. Water pools in areas that clearly used to drain. When you stand on a cracked area it moves under you. Every one of those symptoms means the failure is below the asphalt, in the base or the subgrade, and nothing you put on top will hold. Alligatored areas need to be cut out, the base excavated and rebuilt with compacted stone, and new asphalt placed. If that is a couple of patches, a contractor can do it and then overlay the whole drive. If it is most of the driveway, the honest answer is removal and replacement, and you will find the figures in <a href="/asphalt-driveway-cost/">asphalt driveway cost</a>. A sealer or a resurfacer on this driveway is money you will not get back.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. What Each Costs
        </h2>

        <p>
          These are broad ranges for a typical residential driveway of five to eight hundred square feet, and they move with your region, your access and the state of the surface.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Sealer:</strong> roughly fifteen to fifty cents per square foot. The low end is DIY with a premium retail sealer in two coats, the high end is a contractor with a spray rig and a sanded commercial mix. On a six hundred square foot drive that is a hundred to three hundred dollars. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">the cost of sealing a driveway</a> breaks that down.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>DIY resurfacer product:</strong> fifty cents to a dollar fifty per square foot in material, depending on how thick you have to go and how much of the drive needs it. A pail of trowel-grade resurfacer covers far less area than a pail of sealer, and if you are skimming the whole surface at an eighth of an inch the pails add up fast. Most people resurface the bad thirty or forty per cent and seal the rest, which keeps the cost manageable. Budget for the sealer coats on top as well.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Professional overlay:</strong> three to seven dollars per square foot. That covers cleaning, patching failed areas, tack coat, an inch and a half to two inches of hot mix, compaction and edge work. Milling at the garage and street transitions, extensive base repair and difficult access push it towards the top of the range and beyond. On six hundred square feet you are looking at roughly two to four thousand dollars.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Full removal and replacement:</strong> seven to fifteen dollars per square foot. Tear-out and disposal of the old asphalt, base inspection and rebuild where needed, and a new two- to three-inch mat, sometimes in two lifts. Four to nine thousand dollars for a typical drive, more where the base needs serious work or drainage has to be corrected.
        </p>

        <p>
          The trap is in the middle. A driveway that genuinely needs an overlay can eat five hundred dollars of DIY resurfacer and another two hundred of sealer over three years and end up needing the overlay anyway, having bought nothing but delay. Conversely, a driveway that only needs sealing does not need a resurfacer at any price. Match the treatment to the diagnosis and the cost takes care of itself.
        </p>

        <h2 id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. How Long Each Lasts
        </h2>

        <p>
          A sealer lasts one to three years before it wears thin in the tyre tracks and wants another coat, with the premium sanded and polymer-modified grades at the upper end. It is the shortest-lived treatment because it is the thinnest, and that is fine; it is meant to be a renewable sacrificial layer. Details are in <a href="/how-long-does-driveway-sealer-last/">how long driveway sealer lasts</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          A trowel-grade resurfacer, applied over a sound base and sealed afterwards, holds up for three to six years before the tyre paths start wearing through to the old surface. Keep it sealed on the normal cycle and it goes longer. Where it fails early, the cause is almost always that it was applied over something that was moving, or feathered too thin at the edges where it then lifts.
        </p>

        <p>
          A professional hot mix overlay on a sound base lasts twelve to twenty years, and it is the closest thing to a new driveway you can get without paying for one. Its lifespan depends almost entirely on what is underneath. On a good base with proper drainage it will outlast the mortgage; on a weak base it will crack along the same lines as the old surface within two or three winters, because the overlay is only as good as the layer it bonds to. A full replacement with a new base is good for twenty to thirty years and is the only option that resets the clock completely.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sequencing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. The Right Order of Work
        </h2>

        <p>
          Whatever you decide the driveway needs, the sequence is fixed: repair, then resurface, then seal. Each step goes over the one before it, and doing them out of order wastes the later ones.
        </p>

        <p>
          Repair first. Clean out and fill every crack wider than an eighth of an inch with a proper filler, using a pourable or trowel-grade product for the wide ones as covered in <a href="/how-to-fill-large-cracks-in-asphalt-driveway/">filling large cracks</a>. Cut out and patch any holes or crumbled edges with <a href="/cold-patch-asphalt/">cold patch</a> or hot mix, and compact the patch properly. Kill any weeds coming through and treat oil stains, because nothing bonds to oil. Let the repairs cure for the time on the label, which for most crack fillers is a day or two and for cold patch can be several weeks of traffic before it is firm enough to coat.
        </p>

        <p>
          Resurface second, if the driveway needs it. Skim the ravelled and depressed areas with trowel-grade resurfacer, working it into the texture and feathering the edges so there is no lip. Do not try to build more than about a quarter inch in one pass; if a depression is deeper than that, fill it in two applications with a cure between them. Then wait. The resurfacer has to cure fully before a sealer goes over it, which is a few days in warm weather and can be a couple of weeks if it is cool or the material is thick. Sealing over an uncured resurfacer traps water in it and both layers stay soft.
        </p>

        <p>
          Seal last. Two coats of a good sealer over the repaired, resurfaced, cleaned and fully dry surface. If the driveway got a hot mix overlay rather than a trowelled product, the sealing waits much longer: new asphalt needs six to twelve months for its lighter oils to flash off before it is ready to take a sealer, and coating it early makes the sealer soft and prone to scuffing. See <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a>. After that, the driveway goes on the normal <a href="/driveway-resealing/">resealing cycle</a>, and the resurfacer or overlay underneath stays protected for its full life.
        </p>

        <h2 id="money-wasted" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          8. When Resurfacing Is Money Wasted
        </h2>

        <p>
          Everything laid on top of an asphalt driveway, from the thinnest sealer to a two-inch overlay, depends on what is underneath staying still. Asphalt is a flexible pavement; it has almost no strength of its own and it works by spreading loads down into a compacted stone base and a firm subgrade. When that base is weak, waterlogged, poorly compacted or washed out, the asphalt above it flexes every time a car rolls over, and it cracks in the characteristic alligator pattern, then breaks up into potholes. No surface treatment changes that. A resurfacer skimmed over an alligatored patch cracks along the same lines within one winter. An overlay laid over a failed base reflects the old cracks through in two or three years and you have paid for a new surface that is failing on schedule.
        </p>

        <p>
          The tells are the ones in the third diagnostic group: alligatoring, heaving, sinking, potholes that recur after patching, edges that have dropped, and any area that moves when you stand on it. Add to that list a driveway where water runs towards the house or pools against the garage slab, because that is a drainage problem the base will keep suffering from until it is corrected, and a driveway on a slope with a soft shoulder that is slowly sliding sideways. On these driveways, the right sequence is base repair first, by cutting out the failed areas down to the stone and rebuilding them, and only then a surface. Sometimes that is a few patches and an overlay. Sometimes it is a new driveway. Either way, a contractor who looks at alligatoring and offers to resurface it without cutting anything out is offering you a product with a two-year life at a fifteen-year price, and a bag of trowel-grade resurfacer on the same problem is the cheaper way of wasting the same afternoon.
        </p>

        <p>
          The last trap is the elderly driveway that is simply done. Asphalt that is twenty-five or thirty years old, has been sealed a dozen times, and has a surface that is more filler than pavement will keep absorbing sealer and resurfacer indefinitely, and it will keep coming apart, because the binder has oxidised through its full depth and the material has no cohesion left. At that point the honest advice is to stop spending on maintenance products and put the money towards replacement.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Expecting sealer to fill anything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It is thinner than a credit card. It protects sound asphalt and does nothing for cracks, dips or loose stones except make them black.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Resurfacing over alligator cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Alligatoring is base failure. Any coating or overlay placed on top cracks in the same pattern within a season or two. Cut it out and rebuild the base first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing before the resurfacer has cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A thick resurfacer holds water for days. Seal over it too early and both layers stay soft and scuff under the first car. Wait the full cure time on the pail, and longer in cool weather.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Stacking coats of filler-sealer to level a dip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Four coats of sealer is an expensive, soft, slow-drying way of doing what one pass of trowel-grade resurfacer does properly. Use the right material for the depth.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping the crack repair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Cracks under a resurfacer or sealer keep moving and telegraph straight through. Every crack wider than an eighth of an inch gets filled before anything goes over it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between driveway sealer and driveway resurfacer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A sealer is a thin protective coating that shields sound asphalt from sun, water and oxidation; it repairs nothing. A resurfacer is a much thicker, aggregate-loaded material that fills ravelled texture, spider cracks and shallow depressions to create a new wearing surface. A professional overlay, an inch and a half to two inches of new hot mix, is the heavy-duty version of resurfacing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to seal my driveway after resurfacing it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">With a DIY trowel-grade resurfacer, yes, once it has fully cured, usually after a few days to a couple of weeks depending on the product. With a hot mix overlay, wait six to twelve months for the new asphalt to cure before sealing. In both cases the sealer protects the investment you just made.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to resurface a driveway versus sealing it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Sealing runs roughly fifteen to fifty cents per square foot. A DIY resurfacer product costs about fifty cents to a dollar fifty per square foot in material. A professional hot mix overlay is typically three to seven dollars per square foot, and full removal and replacement seven to fifteen. The right one depends on what is actually wrong with the driveway.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you resurface a driveway with alligator cracks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Not successfully with a coating. Alligator cracking means the base under the asphalt has failed, and any resurfacer or overlay laid over it will crack in the same pattern within a season or two. Those areas need to be cut out, the base repaired and new asphalt placed before anything goes over the top.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a driveway resurfacer last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A trowel-grade DIY resurfacer over a sound base lasts three to six years before it starts to wear through in the tyre tracks, and longer if it is sealed and maintained. A professional hot mix overlay lasts twelve to twenty years on a good base. A sealer alone lasts one to three years.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a filler-sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A filler-sealer, sold as a filled or fortified sealer, is an asphalt emulsion with extra sand and mineral filler so that it lays down a thicker film than a plain sealer. It fills hairline texture and minor roughness better than a plain sealer, but it is still a coating, not a resurfacer, and it will not level depressions or bridge open cracks.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I patch cracks before resurfacing or sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Always. Any crack wider than about an eighth of an inch should be cleaned and filled with a proper crack filler, and holes or crumbling edges should be patched with cold patch or hot mix. Sealer and resurfacer both go over repaired asphalt, never in place of the repair.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">When is resurfacing a waste of money?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">When the problem is under the asphalt rather than in it. Heaving, sinking, alligator cracking, potholes that keep coming back and edges that have dropped all point to base failure. Any material laid on top inherits the movement and fails quickly. Those driveways need base repair or replacement, not a new surface.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

